menu "breadboard: bb_timer"

config BB_TIMER_DISP_STACK
    int "Shared dispatcher task stack size (bytes)"
    default 4096
    range 2048 16384
    help
        Stack for the bb_timer_disp task that runs MODE A deferred
        work functions. mdns/cache/cJSON are safe; TLS is not (use MODE B).

config BB_TIMER_DISP_PRIORITY
    int "Shared dispatcher task priority"
    default 5
    range 1 20
    help
        FreeRTOS priority for the bb_timer_disp task. Default 5: above
        background worker tasks (1) and equal to bb_event_disp (5), below
        the esp_timer service task (~22).

config BB_TIMER_DISP_CORE
    int "Shared dispatcher task core affinity (-1 = no affinity)"
    default -1
    range -1 1
    help
        Core to pin the bb_timer_disp task to. Default -1 (no affinity) lets
        the scheduler place it on whichever core has CPU. Without affinity,
        the prio-5 dispatcher cannot be starved on boards where core 1 runs
        a high-priority CPU-bound task (e.g. mining at prio 20). Boards that
        specifically want it off the esp_timer service core (core 0) can still
        pin it explicitly via CONFIG_BB_TIMER_DISP_CORE.

        A dispatcher that is merely starved -- not hung -- for longer than
        CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S trips BB_TIMER_DISP_WDT_ENABLE's same
        path as a genuine hang. Today, with CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_PANIC unset
        by default, that only produces a spurious warning; a board owner who
        later enables CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_PANIC should check the core and
        priority layout here first, or a starvation spell can trigger an
        unwanted reboot.

config BB_TIMER_DISP_QUEUE_DEPTH
    int "Shared dispatcher coalescing queue depth"
    default 16
    range 4 64
    help
        Depth of the internal queue between signal-only timer callbacks
        and the bb_timer_disp worker task. With coalescing, one slot per
        timer is sufficient in practice; the queue is a safety net for
        burst scenarios.

config BB_TIMER_DISP_WDT_ENABLE
    bool "Subscribe bb_timer_disp to the Task Watchdog Timer"
    default y
    depends on ESP_TASK_WDT_EN
    help
        When enabled, the shared dispatcher task (bb_timer_disp) subscribes
        to the global Task WDT at startup and feeds it on every idle timeout
        and after each work_fn returns.

        A work_fn that blocks longer than CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S
        (the global Task WDT timeout) trips the TWDT ISR, which logs the
        stuck task by name. With CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_PANIC unset -- the
        default in every sdkconfig shipped in this repo -- that is ALL it
        does: the ISR re-arms and the dispatcher stays wedged exactly as
        before, just no longer silent. This is NOT a reboot and NOT
        self-recovery; it only gets you a reboot with reset_reason task_wdt
        if the board also has CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_PANIC enabled.

        The warning is expected to stay visible even if bb_log itself is
        wedged, since the TWDT ISR logs via a direct ROM-UART print path
        that bypasses bb_log's writer queue -- not through the same queue a
        stuck dispatcher could be blocking.

        The effective stall-to-reboot bound (when PANIC is also on) is
        CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S, not BB_TIMER_DISP_WDT_FEED_MS. The
        feed interval only controls how often the dispatcher feeds on idle
        (queue empty) ticks; it must be shorter than the global WDT timeout.

        Default y — bb_timer_disp runs every MODE_SHARED work_fn
        synchronously with no per-callback timeout of its own, so a single
        hung callback previously parked every periodic job on the
        dispatcher silently forever: no warning, no reset_reason, nothing
        to attribute a hang to. Subscribing it to the Task WDT gives that
        hang a name in the log, even though the dispatcher itself remains
        wedged unless CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_PANIC is also on. Turn this off
        only if even the attributed warning is unwanted.

config BB_TIMER_DISP_WDT_FEED_MS
    int "Dispatcher WDT idle feed interval (ms)"
    default 5000
    range 1000 30000
    depends on BB_TIMER_DISP_WDT_ENABLE
    help
        How often the bb_timer_disp task feeds the Task WDT when the
        dispatch queue is empty (idle). Must be shorter than
        CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S. Default 5000 ms.

endmenu
